Absolutely. If you want the name to feel 1950s Soviet nerdy rather than simply “Soviet + programming,” there’s a surprisingly rich vocabulary to mine: early computers, cybernetics, mathematics, space technology, radio, engineering, and Soviet scientific institutions.

The sweet spot for a coding-problem site is something that sounds like it could be a real Soviet scientific project, while still being memorable as a domain.

My strongest ideas
SovietCode — very clear, but perhaps too generic.
SputnikCode — instantly 1950s USSR + technology; probably my favorite conceptually.
BESM — Bolshaya Elektronno-Schetnaya Mashina (“Large Electronic Computing Machine”), one of the major early Soviet computer families. besm... has a wonderfully nerdy sound.
Strela — the Strela was one of the first Soviet general-purpose computers, developed in the 1950s. As a site name, StrelaCode or simply Strela is excellent.
URAL — Soviet computer series beginning in the 1950s. UralCode sounds surprisingly natural.
M-20 — a famous Soviet computer from the late 1950s. M20Code has a nice minimalist/programmer feel.
Kvant / Quantum — very Soviet-science flavored, although less specifically computing.
Cybernetics — Soviet cybernetics became a fascinating field in the 1950s, despite initially being politically controversial.
Algol — ALGOL was developed in the late 1950s and is one of the foundational programming languages. AlgolCode would be wonderfully nerdy, though not specifically Soviet.
Kosmos — more space-age than computer-age, but extremely evocative.
Sputnik — probably the most immediately recognizable 1950s Soviet technology reference.
Akademgorodok — technically founded in 1957, and associated with Soviet scientific culture. AkademiCode could be a fun derivative.
The really nerdy computer-history rabbit hole

If you want something that programmers/history nerds might recognize, I'd look particularly closely at these:

BESM

BESM was a family of Soviet computers whose first machine, BESM-1, was completed in the early 1950s.

The acronym comes from:

Bolshaya Elektronno-Schetnaya Mashina

roughly, “Large Electronic Computing Machine.”

This gives you some fun possibilities:

BESM.dev
BESMCode
BESM50
BESMProblems
BESM Online
BESM Challenge
BESM Lab

BESM by itself is particularly nice because it looks like an obscure programming acronym rather than a themed website.

Strela

Strela was a Soviet computer developed in the 1950s.

And Strela means “arrow” in Russian.

That's a really nice property for a programming site: it has a technical historical reference, but doesn't scream the theme at first glance.

Possible names:

Strela
StrelaCode
Strela Labs
Strela Computing
Strela Problems
Strela Challenge

I particularly like Strela as the name of a site, with the Soviet-computing connection becoming part of the site's lore.

M-20

The Soviet M-20 computer was developed under Sergei Lebedev's direction and became operational around 1958.

This has a very hacker-y aesthetic:

M20
M20 Code
M20 Problems
M20 Online
m20.dev
m20code.com

It also doesn't lock you into an overt USSR aesthetic.

Ural

The URAL series was another major line of Soviet computers.

This one has a fantastic naming advantage:

URAL already sounds like a proper project name.

UralCode
Ural Problems
Ural Online
Ural Computing
URAL-1
URAL-2

And of course the Ural Mountains give it an additional geographical meaning.

If you want “Advent of Code” energy

I think the key is not to literally put USSR into the name.

USSRofCode.com communicates the joke, but it has a slightly awkward rhythm. Compare:

Advent of Code
Everybody Codes
Strela
BESM
Ural Code
M20

The latter names feel more like a thing that exists, which I think is what you're after.

You could also use the Soviet scientific naming convention as the gimmick:

Problems of M-20
BESM Challenges
Strela Problems
URAL Computing
Sputnik Computing
Academy of Code
Institute of Computation
Computing in the USSR
Soviet Computing
Ministry of Code
People's Computing
Comrade Code
Code of the Soviets
Codeviets ← deliberately silly
Code Academy ← potentially too generic
Academy of Algorithms
Institute of Algorithms
Some more playful ones

If you want the site to have a little personality:

Comrade Code
Comrade Compiler
Comrade Programmer
Red Code
Red Algorithm
Red Machine
Red Computer
Code Sputnik
Sputnik Algorithms
Sputnik Computing
Soviet Algorithms
Socialist Computing
People's Computer
People's Algorithm
Five-Year Plan
Five-Year Program
Plan.exe
Gosplan.exe
Ministry of Algorithms
Ministry of Computation
Central Committee of Code

Five-Year Program is particularly fun because “program” has both the political and programming meanings.

And some deep-cut references

If you want the name to reward people who know Soviet computer history:

Lebedev — Sergei Lebedev was a foundational figure in Soviet computing.
Lebedev Machine — extremely nerdy and historically meaningful.
MESM — Malaya Elektronnaya Schetnaya Mashina, “Small Electronic Computing Machine.” The MESM was an important early Soviet computer.
Minsk — Soviet computers including the Minsk series.
Setun — a very unusual Soviet ternary computer developed around 1958–1959. This one is GOLD for a coding site.
Setun Code
Setun.dev
Setun Problems
Ternary — a reference to Setun's ternary architecture, and a wonderfully nerdy programming name.
BESM-6 — later than your 1950s target, but hugely important in Soviet computing history.
Dnepr — another Soviet computer/project name.
Minsk-1
M-1 — one of the early Soviet computers.
Institute of Precision Mechanics and Computer Technology — the sort of gloriously bureaucratic Soviet institution you could parody for branding.

Setun deserves special attention. Setun was a Soviet computer designed around ternary rather than binary computing. That's exactly the kind of obscure historical nerd reference that could make an excellent programming-challenge brand.

For example:

Setun
Programming problems from another timeline.

That's a pretty compelling identity.

My shortlist

If I were actually choosing the brand, I'd investigate these first:

Name	Nerdiness	1950s USSR	Brandability
Strela	★★★★★	★★★★★	★★★★★
Setun	★★★★★	★★★★★	★★★★★
BESM	★★★★★	★★★★★	★★★★☆
M20	★★★★★	★★★★★	★★★★☆
Ural Code	★★★★☆	★★★★★	★★★★★
Sputnik Code	★★★★☆	★★★★★	★★★★★
Comrade Code	★★★☆☆	★★★★★	★★★★☆
Five-Year Program	★★★★☆	★★★★★	★★★★☆
Red Algorithm	★★★☆☆	★★★★☆	★★★★☆
USSR of Code	★★☆☆☆	★★★★★	★★☆☆☆

My personal top 3:

🥇 Strela — elegant, obscure, historically real, and “arrow” is a nice metaphor for solving problems.
🥈 Setun — extremely nerdy; the ternary-computer connection gives you lots of possibilities for the site's identity.
🥉 BESM — unmistakably computer-historical and has the right weird-acronym flavor.

If you're aiming specifically for the “Advent of Code / Everybody Codes” level of catchy, I'd lean toward Strela or Setun, rather than trying to construct a phrase containing “USSR” and “Code.”
